## 7. Milestones

### Phase 0: Setup (Day 1-2)
- [ ] Create the repo with README, LICENSE (MIT), `.gitignore`
- [ ] Set up `pyproject.toml`, ruff, mypy, pytest
- [ ] Add a GitHub Actions workflow that runs lint and tests

### Phase 1: Skeleton (Week 1)
- [ ] Define the provider interface (`generate(request) -> response`)
- [ ] Implement two adapters (one hosted API, one local via Ollama)
- [ ] Build the `/v1/chat/completions` endpoint (non-streaming)
- [ ] Load model list, prices, and limits from YAML
- [ ] **Done when:** a request can be sent to a chosen model through the router

### Phase 2: Basic routing (Week 2)
- [ ] Preprocessor: token count, code detection, vision detection
- [ ] Rule-based strategy driven by config
- [ ] Fallback chain with timeouts and retries
- [ ] Unit tests for each rule and failure case
- [ ] **Done when:** different prompts reach different models, and a failing provider triggers fallback

### Phase 3: Observability (Week 3)
- [ ] Log each request: model, reason, tokens, latency, cost, status
- [ ] Add a `/metrics` or `/stats` endpoint
- [ ] Add streaming support
- [ ] **Done when:** total cost and per-model usage can be reported

### Phase 4: Smarter routing (Week 4-5)
- [ ] Complexity scorer (heuristic first, small classifier later)
- [ ] Cascade strategy with a quality check
- [ ] Strategy selection via config (`strategy: rules | complexity | cascade`)

### Phase 5: Evaluation (Week 5-6)
- [ ] Build an eval set (50-200 prompts with easy, medium, hard labels)
- [ ] Script that runs every strategy on the eval set
- [ ] Compare **quality vs. cost vs. latency** against two baselines: always-cheap and always-best
- [ ] Publish results in `docs/results.md`
- [ ] **Done when:** there is a chart or table showing the router beats "always-best" on cost at similar quality

### Phase 6: Polish and release (Week 7)
- [ ] Docker image and docker-compose example
- [ ] Complete README: quickstart, config guide, architecture diagram
- [ ] `CONTRIBUTING.md` and issue templates
- [ ] Tag `v0.1.0`

### Later ideas
- Learned router trained on logged data
- Semantic caching to skip repeat calls
- Per-user or per-team budgets and rate limits
- Simple web dashboard
- Support for tool calling and structured outputs across providers

